{"name":"Miles","hex":"#829ba0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Miles","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A male given name from an uncertain origin.","A surname originating as a patronymic."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Miles"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mile","blocks":[{"text":"The mile, sometimes the international mile or statute mile to distinguish it from other miles, is a British imperial unit and United States customary unit of length; both are based on the older English unit of length equal to 5,280 English feet, or 1,760 yards. The statute mile was standardised as a unit of length between the Commonwealth of Nations and the United States by an international agreement in 1959, when the yard was formally redefined with respect to SI units as 0.9144 metres, making the mile exactly 1609.344 metres. For everyday use, five miles equates roughly to eight kilometres. Mileage is the term used informally for a distance expressed in miles."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Milestone%2C_Knightsbridge%2C_London_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1590514.jpg/330px-Milestone%2C_Knightsbridge%2C_London_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1590514.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mile","wikidata":"Q253276"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/RomaForoRomanoMiliariumAureum.JPG/500px-RomaForoRomanoMiliariumAureum.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/RomaForoRomanoMiliariumAureum.JPG/1280px-RomaForoRomanoMiliariumAureum.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/RomaForoRomanoMiliariumAureum.JPG/500px-RomaForoRomanoMiliariumAureum.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The supposed remains of the Golden Milestone, the zero-mile marker of the Roman road network, in the Roman Forum","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARomaForoRomanoMiliariumAureum.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Mercator_scale.png/500px-Mercator_scale.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Mercator_scale.png/1280px-Mercator_scale.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Mercator_scale.png/500px-Mercator_scale.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Scalebar on a 16th-century map made by Mercator. The scalebar is expressed in \"Hours walking or common Flemish miles\", and includes three actual scales: small, medium and big Flemish miles.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMercator_scale.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Wegmasse1.png/500px-Wegmasse1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Wegmasse1.png/500px-Wegmasse1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Various historic miles and leagues from an 1848 German textbook, given in feet, metres, and fractions of a \"degree of meridian\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWegmasse1.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Milestone%2C_Mountbellow_%28geograph_5365674%29.jpg/500px-Milestone%2C_Mountbellow_%28geograph_5365674%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Milestone%2C_Mountbellow_%28geograph_5365674%29.jpg/500px-Milestone%2C_Mountbellow_%28geograph_5365674%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedi